 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Probably yes, with some caution. The latest reading I found is the morning one, so check again before you go out.**\n\n- **Latest reading:** All 21 air quality monitoring stations across Sarawak recorded \"Moderate\" API readings at 8am on Friday (Oct 16). Kuching was at 72.\n- **Trend:** It has improved. On Oct 14, Lubok Antu, Serian, Sri Aman and Tebedu were still in the unhealthy range, above 100. Kuching itself had reached an unhealthy 111 before dropping to 85 around Oct 11.\n- **What \"moderate\" means:** An API of 51 to 100 is moderate, and 101 to 200 is unhealthy. Malaysia's guidance is that outdoor activities are postponed when the API exceeds 100. At 72, Kuching is well under that.\n\n**Advice for this evening**\n- **Check the live API first.** Use the NREB Sarawak or DOE APIMS portal or app. Haze can change within hours, and I can't see the current reading.\n- **Cycling is fine if the API is under about 100.** Moderate air is generally acceptable for most healthy people. Keep the ride easy, because hard efforts mean you breathe more deeply and take in more particulates.\n- **Ride at a lower intensity, or skip it, if any of these apply:**\n  - The API rises above 100.\n  - You have asthma, heart or lung conditions.\n  - You're pregnant.\n  - You get throat irritation, coughing, chest tightness or eye irritation. If so, stop and go indoors.\n- **A cloth or surgical mask won't filter fine haze particles well.** A properly fitted N95 or KN95 does, but it makes hard cycling harder.\n- **Choose quieter routes** away from heavy traffic, which adds to your exposure.\n\nIf the API is still in the 70s or lower when you leave, a normal evening ride should be fine.",
